> Dear All
> First, thank you for the webinar introducing your new publication The Macroeconomic Case for Investing in Climate Adaptation. I will read it with interest. I regularly review papers on Linkedin under the title Just a Thought. 
> 
> Second, allow me to briefly introduce myself. I am a political economist, British but have been living and working in Hong Kong since 1989. Before my semi-retirement I was Associate Prof at the University of Hong Kong. My original focus of scholarship was on telecoms and digital transformations, issues that were emerging during the 1990s, but these are no longer such pressing issues. Today climate change and loss of biodiversity are the existential threats. Since 2023 I have published a book on Achieving the UN SDGs (Emerald Publications, 2024) and Climate Change and Carbon Markets (Springer Nature, Jan 2026). 
> 
> I was the person asking the question as to whether an independent International Green Bank (IGB) would be a vehicle towards scaling up financial support for mitigation and adaptation. Maybe by adding "independent" I inadvertently gave the impression this would be yet another private bank. On the contrary, I argue that it would partially displace multilateral development banks and the World Bank in two respects: (i) it would be governed by govts (especially those from the Global South such as China and India), by private sector participants, and by civil society organisations, completely distinct from ("independent" of) existing institutions; (ii) it would absorb, subsume and/or share existing financing exclusively for the Global South, for mitigation and adaptation and biodiversity projects. Geopolitics and entrenched interests would make it very difficult to swing support behind such a proposal, but I came to the conclusion, after researching the second book, that an IGB would be the only clear path forward. The existing IFA is simply insufficient, based as it is on the catalytic model. It is necessary but insufficient. I would invite you to see my paper on an IGB at: 
> https://johnure.net/2026/02/13/an-international-green-bank/
> 
> Only after I had written the paper I discovered it had antecedents, including economist Kenneth Rogoff who in 2019 suggested a Carbon Bank, an idea that apparently was picked up by Hank Paulson and forwarded to China's financial czar, before COVID killed off the idea. It has also been proposed by Hafez Ghamen, an ex-regional director of the World Bank - see reference in my paper. And I discovered last week, it has been proposed by Prof Jayant Sinha who I believe has taught at the LSE? [https://www.orfonline.org/research/a-green-bank-for-global-south-climate-finance ] 
> 
> In a nutshell, I believe that an IGB could (i) scale and focus climate finance in a way that is not currently possible with MDBs because they have such diverse portfolios; (ii) it would directly engage the Global South in its governance in a way that is either lacking today or is fragmented (e.g., Belt & Road initiative, AIIB, etc. ); (iii) in addition to mitigation and adaptation, it would foster in-country training and development programmes in areas such as monitoring, reporting and verification standards for in-country projects (a skill set that remains sadly lacking in most EMDEs leaving the market to expensive consultants (l invite you to check out https://johnure.net/2026/02/17/concept-note-monitoring-reporting-verifying-mrv/ for some early thoughts on this) and adaptation design and management. The effects would help reduce risk and the cost of capital in local markets. 
> 
> If you have the time (15 minute read) and interest (it relates directly to the issues you raised) I would greatly appreciate your thoughts.
> 
> Very best regards
> 
> John Ure
